+
+## Developer diagnostics
+
+The homepage has independent, click-to-connect checks for a hardware device on
+the reader's private IPv4 LAN and a companion CLI on IPv4 loopback. Readers can
+edit the full HTTP(S) URL, port, and path; the defaults are examples, not bundled
+services. Both services can be checked independently in the same session.
+
+Each check sends a direct browser `GET` with CORS, no cookies, no redirects, and
+no caching. A readable HTTP 2xx response verifies reachability; no response body
+schema is required and no persistent connection is opened. Checks can be canceled
+and time out after 15 seconds. Addresses and results are not persisted or sent
+through the site's server.
+
+Configure each endpoint to allow the deployed site's origin using
+`Access-Control-Allow-Origin` (the origin excludes `/devtools-times/`). Use a
+read-only health endpoint such as `/health`. The hardware service must listen on
+its LAN interface and be reachable through its firewall; the companion CLI can
+bind to `127.0.0.1`. This widget does not install or start either service.
+
+Use the site over HTTPS or a trustworthy localhost development origin. Supporting
+browsers handle local network permission prompts when Connect is clicked. Requests
+specify `targetAddressSpace: 'local'` for hardware and `'loopback'` for the CLI.
+Browser support varies: browsers without local HTTP access support may require an
+HTTPS endpoint with a trusted certificate. Denied permissions can be changed in
+browser site settings. See [Chrome's local network access guidance](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/modern-web-guidance/guides/security/local-network-access).
